

4. Underlying Medical Conditions

Sometimes, a noticeable spike in heart rate after eating points to an underlying health efficiency or condition:

• Gastrocardiac Syndrome (Roemheld Syndrome): Gas or bloating in the stomach or abdomen can physically press against the diaphragm, irritating the vagus nerve and triggering heart palpitations or an increased heart rate.

• POTS (Postural Orthostatic Tachycardia Syndrome): People with POTS often experience a severe pooling of blood in the abdomen after eating, leading to a dramatic, uncomfortable spike in heart rate.

• Acid Reflux or GERD: The discomfort or burning of acid reflux can mimic chest tightness or stimulate nerves that cause your heart to feel like it is racing.

• Anemia or Thyroid Issues: An overactive thyroid (hyperthyroidism) or low red blood cell count can make your cardiovascular system hyper-reactive to normal stresses like digestion.




